course: dictionary
lesson: 13767
title: Intersection: where things meet and cross
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An intersection is a place where two or more things meet or cross. Most often it is a place where roads cross, like a crossroads or junction. «We waited at the busy intersection before we crossed the road.» The word busy tells you the crossing is full of traffic, so an intersection is a meeting point for roads.
explain: The word also has a maths meaning. When two lines cross, the point where they meet is their intersection. «The two lines cross at one point, the intersection.» Clue: the lines meet, so the intersection is the spot where they touch.
explain: In a Venn diagram, the intersection is the middle part where two circles overlap. It holds the things that belong to both sets. «Sam likes football and swimming, so he goes in the intersection of the two circles.» Clue: he belongs to both circles, so he is in the overlap.
explain: To work out the meaning, ask: are two roads, lines or sets crossing or meeting? If yes, it is an intersection. Then put your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: An intersection is a place where two or more roads meet or cross.
fact: In road safety, you must stop, look and cross carefully at an intersection.
fact: In maths, the intersection of two lines is the point where they cross.
fact: In a Venn diagram, the intersection is where the circles overlap: the things that belong to both.
fact: Useful phrases: «at the intersection», «the point of intersection», «busy intersection».
exercise: «We stopped at the busy intersection and looked both ways.» What is an intersection here? | A loud noise from a car | *A place where two roads meet or cross | A kind of bicycle | A quiet field with no roads
exercise: In a Venn diagram, where do you write the things that belong to both sets? | *In the intersection, where the circles overlap | At the very top of the page | In the empty outside part of the page | In the title box
exercise: Two lines cross at one point. What is that point called? | A border | *The intersection | A sentence | A circle
test: «Be careful at the intersection: look left, look right, then cross.» What must you do at an intersection? | *Stop, look and cross carefully | Run across without looking | Ring a bell | Sit down and wait for an hour
test: In maths, what is the intersection of two lines? | *The point where they cross | The longest side of the page | A new line that never touches | The number of lines drawn
exam: Which best describes an intersection in a Venn diagram? | A number that is always zero | The title at the top | The outside edge of the box | *The overlap where two circles meet, holding things in both sets
